// @run
// @log 1
// @log 7
// THE `.map` CLOSURE-VALUE CALLBACK IS THE FOURTH CONSUMER OF THE `$fnsig` POOL, and this
// file is the only thing in the tree that says so.
//
// D351's landing routes every pool position through ONE resolver (`cloSigTypeIdxAt`) so a
// closure's DECLARED functype and every `call_indirect` operand naming it move together.
// Four sites ask: the function section's declared type, the value call, the arity fallback,
// and this one — `.map`/`.filter` handed a callback that is a closure VALUE rather than a
// named function, which resolves the sig from the callback's own `$fnsig` key.
//
// IT SCORES ZERO ON EVERY POPULATION THE REPO HAS. The 210-cell D199 grid never reaches
// this site (`Mreach=0`), the distilled corpus never reaches it, and over all 1,919 buildable
// `tests/cases` modules it is reached 8 times and its answer never changes. That is why the
// file exists: with the `.map` site left unrouted and the other three routed, this program
// stops running — `mapWith`'s `call_indirect` names the pool entry `s0;>i` while the lambda
// it dispatches to is declared at that entry's byte-identical twin `V0;>i`, and the engine
// traps `indirect call type mismatch` after the first line has printed.
//
// The duplicate is D280's cross-table merge, the same one the union sibling
// `arm-decl-twin-fnsig-pool-dedup.vl` pins: arm `Circle` and declared struct `Dot` share one
// heap type, so `(structref, (ref $h)) -> i32` is rendered by two different keys. `useArm`
// interns the ARM spelling first, so the `.map` callback's STRUCT spelling is the twin.
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
type Dot = { r: i32 }
function useArm(g: (Circle) => i32, c: Circle) { print(g(c)) }
useArm((p: Circle) => p.r, { r: 1 })
function mapWith(h: (Dot) => i32, xs: Dot[]) {
  const out = xs.map(h)
  print(out[0])
}
mapWith((q: Dot) => q.r, [{ r: 7 }])
